### Part VII — Implementation order and deployment

### 20\. Build sequence

The design team and infrastructure team should work in parallel, rather than wait for the entire backend migration before improving the frontend.

### Implementation dependency roadmap

A–H represent sequential development milestones, not fixed weeks or delivery estimates.

| Milestone | Required outcome                                           |
| --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| A         | Verified sources, licenses, baseline and rollback          |
| B         | Original design system and companion renderer              |
| C         | New workspace interface and hardened core integration      |
| D         | Isolated computers, durable work and scoped credentials    |
| E         | Interactive output, communications and device connectivity |
| F         | Complete verified ecosystem adapters                       |
| G         | Commercial tenancy, entitlements and billing               |
| H         | Independently verified production release                  |

### Server considerations

Your current server has 8 vCPUs, approximately 23 GiB RAM, no detected dedicated GPU and a substantial number of existing services.

I would use it for development coordination, repository management, staging and controlled integration testing. Large concurrent browser fleets, GPU inference and commercial tenant workloads should ultimately be distributed across isolated workers rather than concentrated on the existing production host.

### 21\. Final definition of done

UAP is not complete merely because the interface looks better.

A fully completed implementation must demonstrate that a user can create an agent, customize its plush companion, issue its identity, provision an isolated computer, connect approved tools, assign work, collaborate with other agents, receive interactive results, inspect memory, communicate across devices and manage authorized resources.

The same agent must retain its identity across all those operations.

| Requirement               | Completion evidence                                        |
| ------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| Original premium design   | Independently reviewed desktop and mobile screenshots      |
| Animated companions       | Runtime-connected rendering and performance tests          |
| Autonomous task execution | Real completed workflows with execution receipts           |
| Computer isolation        | Cross-agent and cross-tenant negative tests                |
| Persistent identity       | SID conformance and lifecycle tests                        |
| Memory                    | Persistence, provenance and deletion verification          |
| Ecosystem integration     | Live cross-system protocol tests                           |
| Communication             | Successful authorized voice, messaging and device sessions |
| Commercial functionality  | Verified subscriptions, usage and billing                  |
| Security                  | Independent assessment and remediated findings             |
| Reliability               | Recovery, cancellation, rollback and restoration tests     |

No production feature should be marked complete on the basis of a mock interface, a passing type check or a directory containing unfinished source code.

